<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishaq ibn Muhammad of Carmona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishaq ibn Muhammad was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> in the mid-11th century, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Birzalid family who had taken the town after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Pressed by the expanding Abbadid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-birzal">Birzalids</a> kept a precarious independence. His son Aziz ibn Ishaq was the last ruler before Seville took Carmona in 1067.</p></div>
